  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A glacier moves $49$ feet in $14$ months, moving at a constant rate. What is the speed of the glacier?",
  "worked_solution": "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, in feet per month, we divide the number of feet $49,$ by the number of months, $14.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{49}{14} & =\\frac{49\u00f77}{14\u00f77} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =7\u00f72 \\\\  & =3.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the glacier is $3.5$ feet per month.",
